          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Guidance on physical activity for type 2 diabetes is in the document \"Physical activity for type 2 diabetes.\" It describes two main types of activity:\n\n- **Aerobic activity:** at least 150 minutes per week of moderate-to-vigorous activity, spread over at least three days. No more than two days in a row should pass without activity. The guidance says this spacing matters for diabetes because the improvement in insulin sensitivity after a session lasts roughly 24 to 72 hours. Activity bunched into one weekend leaves most of the week without that benefit.\n- **Resistance training:** on two to three non-consecutive days each week.\n\nA related guidance document defines moderate activity as breathing harder while still able to talk but not sing, such as brisk walking, cycling on the flat, or swimming. Vigorous activity leaves you unable to say more than a few words without pausing. That document is about high blood pressure, so treat its definitions as general background.\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not cover reducing sedentary time, so I can't tell you what is recommended on that point. The passages also don't give specific guidance on how long to rest between sessions for people with type 2 diabetes beyond the spacing described above. A clinician or a diabetes educator can advise on activity suited to your own health, especially if you take glucose-lowering medicines, since activity can change blood sugar levels.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
